WEBVTT

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:02.800
Thank you for listening.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:46.150
Hello there, Mr. Naff. Working pretty late, aren't you, Mr. Naff?

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:47.840
Wait enough. Let's ride.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.270
You look kind of all in at that.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:54.440
Fine.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:57.050
How's the insurance been, this Mr. Naff?

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:57.880
Okay.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:02:59.960
They wouldn't ever sell...

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:06.410
me and they said i had something loose in my heart aha i say it's rheumatism yeah 12.

00:05:04.156 --> 00:05:04.996
office memorandum.

00:05:06.908 --> 00:05:08.748
Old enough to Barton Keyes, claims manager.

00:05:11.068 --> 00:05:13.068
Los Angeles, July 16th, 1938.

00:05:15.292 --> 00:05:15.792
The keys.

00:05:18.364 --> 00:05:20.024
Suppose you'll call this a confession when you hear it.

00:05:21.468 --> 00:05:21.728
Bye-bye.

00:05:21.852 --> 00:05:23.852
I like the word confession.

00:05:24.188 --> 00:05:27.288
I just want to set you right about something you couldn't see because it was smack up against your nose.

00:05:29.116 --> 00:05:29.996
You think you're such a hot...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.460
Potato is a claims manager, such a wolf on a phony claim.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:34.830
Maybe I are.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.780
Let's take a look at that Diedrichson claim.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:40.720
Accident and double indemnity.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:44.120
You were pretty good in there for a while, Keyes.

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:47.500
He said it wasn't an accident. Check.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:50.760
He said it wasn't suicide. Check.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:53.460
He said it was murder. Check.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.620
He thought you had a cold, didn't you?

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.160
wrapped up in tissue paper with pink ribbons around it.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:04.020
It was perfect.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:07.080
Except it wasn't because you made one mistake.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.700
Just one little mistake.

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:12.760
When it came to picking the killer,

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:13.520
you picked the wrong guy.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:22.180
You want to know who killed Dietrichson? Hold tight to that cheap cigar of yours, Keyes.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:29.850
I killed Dietrichson. Me, Walter Neff, insurance salesman, 35 years old.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:42.020
unmarried no visible scars until a while ago that is yes i killed him killed him for money

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:53.820
for a woman you get the money and you get the woman pretty isn't it

00:07:08.636 --> 00:07:09.816
It all began last May.

00:07:11.932 --> 00:07:12.972
Around the end of me, it was.

00:07:15.196 --> 00:07:17.976
I've been out to Glendale to deliver a policy on some dairy trucks.

00:07:19.324 --> 00:07:22.244
On the way back, I remembered this auto-renewal near Los Feliz Boulevard.

00:07:22.844 --> 00:07:23.744
So I drove over there.

00:07:25.116 --> 00:07:28.916
It was one of those California Spanish houses everyone was nuts about 10 or 15 years ago.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:32.320
This one must have cost somebody about 30,000 bucks.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:35.160
That is, if he ever finished paying for it.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:41.200
Who wants to see him?

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:42.400
The name is Neff, Walter Neff.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:44.540
If you're selling something...

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.000
I want to talk to Mr. Dedrickson. It's not magazine subscription.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:48.760
Listen, Mr. Dedrickson is not in.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:49.820
How soon do you expect him?

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:51.880
He'll be home when he gets here, if that's any help to you.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:53.260
What is it, Nettie? Who is it?

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.200
It's for Mr. Dedrickson.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:57.690
I'm Mrs. Dedrickson. What is it?

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.520
How do you do, Mrs. Dedrickson?

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:01.600
I'm Walter Neff, Pacific All Risk.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:02.800
Pacific All what?

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:04.800
The Pacific All Risk Insurance Company.

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:06.860
It's about some renewals on the automobiles.

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:09.550
I've been trying to contact your husband for the past two weeks,

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:10.390
but he's never in his office.

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:12.300
Is there anything I can do?

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:14.880
The insurance ran out on the 15th.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:16.980
I'd hate to think of your having a smashed fender or something

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.300
while you're not fully covered.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:22.830
Perhaps I know what you mean, Mr. Neff.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.070
I've just been taking a sunbed.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:26.920
No pigeons around, I hope.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:29.960
Now, about those policies, Mrs. Dietrich...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.620
Dixon, I hate to take up your time, but...

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:32.340
Oh, that's all right.

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:34.750
If you wait till I put something on, I'll be right down.

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:38.210
Nettie, show Mr. Neff into the living room.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:42.230
Where would the living room be?

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.710
In there, but they keep the liquor locked up.

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:46.740
That's all right, I'll just carry my own keys.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:58.110
The living room was still stuffy from last night's cigars.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:03.740
windows were closed and the sunshine coming in through the venetian blind showed up the dust in

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:11.160
the air on the piano and a couple of fancy frames were mr. dietrichson and lola his daughter by his

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:17.490
first wife they had a bowl of those little red goldfish on the table behind the big davenport but

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:23.610
to tell you the truth keys i wasn't a whole lot interested in goldfish right then or in auto

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:28.940
renewals or in mr. dietrichson and his daughter lola i was thinking about that dame upstairs and

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:29.860
the way she had looked at me

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:35.720
And I wanted to see her again, close, without that silly staircase between us.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:43.010
How long was I?

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:44.230
Not at all, Mrs. Dietrichson.

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:45.730
Hope I've got my face on straight.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:47.450
Perfect for my money.

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:48.880
Neth is the name, isn't it?

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:50.540
Yeah, two Fs. Like in Philadelphia.

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:51.540
Story.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:52.460
What story?

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:53.440
Philadelphia story.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.400
Suppose we sit down, you tell me about the insurance.

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:58.000
My husband never tells me anything.

00:09:59.240 --> 00:09:59.920
Well, it's on your two-

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:06.180
the uh the sal and the plymouth we've been handling this insurance for mr didrickson for

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:11.760
three years we'd hate to see the policies lapse it's a honey of an anglet you're wearing mr

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:18.260
didrickson as i was saying uh we'd hate to see the policies lapse of course we give him 30 days

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:22.060
that's all we're allowed to give i guess he's been too busy down at long beach in the oil fields

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:25.330
couldn't i catch him at home some evening for a few minutes i suppose so but he's never home

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:29.910
much before eight that's fine with me you're not connected with the automobile club are you no

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.640
for the all-risk, Mrs. Diederiksen. Why?

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:34.670
Somebody from the automobile club has been trying to get him.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:36.260
Do they have a better rate?

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.170
If your husband's a member. No, he isn't.

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:41.850
Well, then he'd have to join the club and pay the membership fee to start with.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:44.620
I never knock the other fellow's merchandise, Mrs. Diederiksen.

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:46.860
The automobile club's fine.

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:48.660
I can do just as well for you, though.

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:50.990
I have a very attractive policy here.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:53.630
Wouldn't take me two minutes to put it in front of your husband.

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.540
For instance, we're writing a new kind of 50% retention feature in the collision coverage.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.720
You're a smart insurance man, aren't you, Mr. Neff?

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:03.170
I've been at it 11 years.

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:04.550
Doing pretty well?

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:05.480
It's a living.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:10.520
You handle just automobile insurance or all kinds?

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:11.120
All kinds.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:13.080
Fire, earthquake, theft, public liability,

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:15.590
group insurance, industrial stuff and so on right down the line.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:17.660
Accident insurance?

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:18.770
Accident insurance?

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:19.690
Sure, Mr. Diedrickson.

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:23.620
Which you tell me was engraved on that anklet.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:26.660
Just my name.

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:28.160
As for instance?

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.110
Phyllis? I think I like that.

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:34.440
But you're not sure.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:36.720
I'd have to drive it around the block a couple of times.

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:40.910
Mr. Neff, why don't you drop by tomorrow evening around 8.30.

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:41.890
He'll be in then.

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:43.650
Who? My husband.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:46.060
You were anxious to talk to him, weren't you?

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:50.220
Yeah, I was, but I'm sort of getting over the idea, if you know what I mean.

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:52.630
There's a speed limit in this state, Mr. Neff.

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:53.930
45 miles an hour.

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:55.570
How fast was that going, officer?

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:57.150
I'd say around 90.

00:11:57.370 --> 00:11:59.680
I suppose you get down off your motorcycle and give me a ticket.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:02.120
Suppose I let you off with a warning this time.

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:03.480
Suppose it doesn't take.

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:06.100
Suppose I have to whack you over the knuckles.

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.640
Suppose I bust out crying and put my head on your shoulder.

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:11.790
Suppose you try putting it on my husband's shoulder.

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:20.060
That tears it. 8.30 tomorrow evening, then.

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:21.350
That's what I suggested.

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:22.990
You'll be here, too?

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:24.810
I guess so. I usually am.

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:26.990
Same chair, same perfume, same anklet.

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:29.020
I wonder if I know what you mean.

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:39.330
wonder if you wonder. It was a hot afternoon and I can still remember the smell of honeysuckle all

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:46.250
along that street. How could I have known that murder can sometimes smell like honeysuckle? Maybe

00:12:46.250 --> 00:12:50.070
you would have known, Keyes, the minute she mentioned accident insurance, but I didn't.

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:57.640
I felt like a million. I went back to the office to see if I had any mail. It was the day you had

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:59.360
that truck driver from Englewood on the carpet.

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:01.060
Remember, Keyes?

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:03.960
Jeanette, Keyes wants to see you.

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:05.520
He's been yelling for you all afternoon.

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:07.490
Is he sore? Just frothing at the mouth a little.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:08.980
Park this for me, will you, sweetheart?

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:21.430
Lopez, you're not kidding anybody with that line of balls.

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:22.820
You're in the jam and you know it.

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:25.080
Says you. All I want is my money.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:27.240
Says you. All you're going to get is the cops.

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:29.860
Oh, hello, Walter. This is Sam Gar-Lopez.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:31.740
Oh, sure, I know Mr. Galopas.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:33.050
Wrote a policy on his truck.

00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:34.910
How are you, Mr. Galopas?

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:37.430
I ain't so good. My truck burned down.

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:39.360
Yeah. Now look, Galopas,

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:42.360
every month hundreds of claims come to this desk.

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:44.760
Some of them are phonies, and I know which ones.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.910
How do I know? Because my little man tells me.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:50.230
What little man?

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.030
The little man in here.

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:54.200
Every time one of these phonies comes along,

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:56.220
he ties knots in my stomach. I can't eat.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:58.280
Yours was one of them, Galopas.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:13:59.580
That's how I knew your claim was crooked.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:01.000
So what did I do?

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:04.060
I sent a tow car over to your garage this afternoon.

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:06.610
And they jacked up that burned-out truck of yours.

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:07.350
And what did they find?

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:10.260
They found what was left of a neat pile of shavings.

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:12.850
What shavings?

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:15.980
The ones you soaked with kerosene and dropped a match on.

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:20.300
Mister, I'm just a poor guy.

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:22.240
Maybe I made a mistake.

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:24.000
That's one way of putting it.

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:27.240
I'm feeling so good, Mr. Keel.

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:29.140
Just a minute. Sign this and you'll feel fine.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:29.960
Sign what?

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.220
It's a waiver on your claim. Right here.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:34.020
Here?

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:39.810
Here. Now you're an honest man again.

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:40.920
Goodbye, Carl Lopez.

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.280
I ain't got no more truck.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.460
26 hundred bucks is a lot of dough where I live.

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:48.440
It's a matter, Carl Lopez. Don't you know how to open the door?

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.590
Just put your hand on the knob. Turn it to the left.

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:54.010
Now pull it toward you.

00:14:57.180 --> 00:14:59.710
Thank you, Mr. Keyes.

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:05.220
What kind of an outfit is this, anyway?

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:08.420
Are we an insurance company or just a bunch of dim-witted amateurs...

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:09.640
to write a policy on a mug like that?

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:11.080
Now, wait a minute, Keys. I don't rate this beef.

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:13.090
I clipped a note of that Garloper's application...

00:15:13.090 --> 00:15:15.230
to have him thoroughly investigated before we accepted the risk.

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:16.790
I know you did, Walter. I'm not beefing at you.

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:18.050
It's the company. It's the way they do things.

00:15:18.190 --> 00:15:19.190
The way they don't do things.

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:21.940
The way they'll write anything just to get it down on the sales sheet.

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:25.480
And I'm the guy that asked this idiot up to my neck in phony claims...

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:27.640
so they won't throw more money out the window than they take in at the door.

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:29.480
Okay, turn the record over. Let's hear the other side.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:29.980
Well, I get...

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:36.380
You're sick of trying to pick up after a gang of fast-talking salesmen dumb enough to sell life insurance to a guy who sleeps in the same bed with four rattlesnakes.

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:39.700
Walter, I've had 26 years of this. Let me tell you, I'm getting...

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:43.670
Yeah, and you loved every minute of it, Keyes. You love it, only you worry about it too darn much.

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:47.680
You and your little man. You're so darn conscientious, you're driving yourself crazy.

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.480
You wouldn't even say today's Tuesday unless you look at the calendar.

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:53.070
Then you check to see if it was this year's or last year's calendar.

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:57.100
Then you find out who printed the calendar and find out if their calendar checked with the World Almanac's calendar.

00:15:57.380 --> 00:15:59.940
Now, that's enough of you, Walter. Now, get out of here.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:17.400
when i throw my desk at you i love you too i really did too you old crab i was yelling your

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:23.050
head off i was sore at everybody you never fooled me with your song and dance not for a second i

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:27.600
kind of always knew that behind the cigar ashes on your vest you had a heart as big as a house

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.060
At my office, there was a message from Mrs. Diedrichsen about the renewals.

00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:35.680
She didn't want me to come tomorrow evening.

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:39.650
She wanted me to come Thursday afternoon at 3.30 instead.

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:43.890
I had a lot of stuff lined up for that Thursday afternoon...

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:46.360
including a trip down to Santa Monica...

00:16:46.360 --> 00:16:48.980
to see a couple of live prospects about some group insurance.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:52.620
But I kept thinking about Phyllis Diedrichsen...

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:55.980
and the way that anklet of hers cut into her leg.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:06.260
Hello, Mr. Neff. Aren't you coming in?

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:08.010
I'm considering it.

00:17:11.120 --> 00:17:12.940
I hope you didn't mind my change from the appointment.

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:14.540
Last night wasn't so convenient.

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:17.620
It's all right. I'm working on my staff collection anyway.

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:20.520
I was just fixing some iced tea. Would you like a glass?

00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:22.370
Unless you've got a bottle of beer that's not working.

00:17:22.840 --> 00:17:25.100
There may be some. I never know what's in the icebox.

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:26.250
Nettie!

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:34.230
Oh, about those renewals, Mr. Neff, I talked to my husband about it.

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:34.960
Oh, you did?

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:37.040
Yes, he'll renew with you, he told me so.

00:17:37.400 --> 00:17:39.380
As a matter of fact, I thought he'd be here this afternoon.

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:40.440
But he's not.

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:41.000
No.

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:42.790
It's terrible.

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:46.290
Nettie! Nettie!

00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:49.410
Oh, I forgot. Today's the maid's day off.

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:53.150
Never mind the beer. I see it'll be fine.

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:54.770
Lemon? Sugar?

00:17:54.950 --> 00:17:55.570
Fix it your way.

00:17:58.020 --> 00:17:59.980
As long as it's the maid's day off, maybe there's something I can do.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:08.520
for you like running the vacuum cleaner fresh these pedal vacuum cleaners not much money but

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:11.900
you learn a lot about life i didn't think you learned it from a correspondence course

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:20.090
why don't you pick up this tea drinking you're not english are you oh californian born right here

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:29.320
in los angeles they say all native californians come from iowa mr neff i make it walter that's

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:35.040
Tell me, Walter, on this insurance, how much commission do you make?

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:36.450
20%. Why?

00:18:36.850 --> 00:18:39.230
Oh, I thought perhaps I could throw a little more business your way.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:40.110
I can always use it.

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:41.710
I was thinking about my husband.

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:44.210
I worry a lot about him down in those oil fields.

00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:45.570
It's very dangerous.

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:46.990
Well, not for an executive, is it?

00:18:47.110 --> 00:18:48.530
Oh, he doesn't just sit behind a desk.

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:50.170
He's right down there with those drilling crews.

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:51.960
It's got me worried sick.

00:18:52.180 --> 00:18:54.460
You mean some dark night a crown block mine fall on him?

00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:55.640
Please don't talk like that.

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:56.380
But that's the idea.

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:59.360
The other day a casing line snapped and caught the foreman.

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:01.700
He's in the hospital with a broken back.

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:02.440
That's bad.

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:04.790
It's got me jittery just thinking about it.

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:07.480
Suppose something like that happened to my husband.

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:08.230
It could.

00:19:09.060 --> 00:19:13.230
Well, don't you think he ought to have accident insurance?

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:13.770
Mm-hmm.

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:17.360
What kind of insurance could he have?

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:19.450
All enough to cover doctors and hospital bills.

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:21.720
Say, $125 a week cash benefit.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:23.680
Indy rate around $50,000 capital sum.

00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:25.960
Capital sum? What's that?

00:19:26.140 --> 00:19:26.860
In case he gets killed.

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:29.610
Maybe I shouldn't have said that.

00:19:31.210 --> 00:19:33.410
I suppose you have to think of everything in your business.

00:19:33.590 --> 00:19:34.630
Well, your husband would understand.

00:19:35.020 --> 00:19:37.180
I'm sure I could sell him on the idea of some accident protection.

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:38.950
Why don't I talk to him about it?

00:19:39.220 --> 00:19:41.140
You could try, but he's pretty tough going.

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:42.710
Well, they're all tough at first.

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:46.040
He has a lot on his mind.

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:47.500
He doesn't seem to want to listen to anything

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:49.600
except maybe a baseball game on the radio.

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:52.800
Sometimes we sit here all evening

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:54.340
and never say a word to each other.

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:55.860
Sounds pretty dull.

00:19:56.830 --> 00:19:58.570
So I just sit and knit.

00:20:00.250 --> 00:20:01.050
Is that what you married him for?

00:20:01.510 --> 00:20:03.470
Maybe I like the way his thumbs hold up the wool.

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:06.250
Any time his thumbs get tired,

00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:09.240
only with me around you wouldn't have to knit.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:10.260
Wouldn't I?

00:20:10.740 --> 00:20:11.440
Bet your life you would.

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:16.600
Rome would get this up on its feet.

00:20:20.910 --> 00:20:22.410
I wanted to ask you something, Walter.

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:27.290
Could I get an accident policy for him

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:28.550
without bothering him at all?

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:29.700
How's that again?

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.080
You wouldn't even have to talk to him.

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:34.700
I have a little allowance of my own.

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:37.440
I could pay for it, and he needn't know anything about it.

00:20:40.540 --> 00:20:41.160
Why shouldn't he know?

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:43.320
Because he doesn't want accident insurance.

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:45.330
He's superstitious about it.

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:47.380
A lot of people lie on it. It's funny, isn't it?

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:49.960
If there was a way to get it like that,

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:51.560
all the worry would be over.

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:54.080
See what I mean, Walter?

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:55.400
Sure, I got good eyesight.

00:20:56.470 --> 00:20:58.470
You mean you want him to have the policy without him knowing it?

00:20:59.250 --> 00:20:59.970
That means without the...

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:03.030
knowing that he doesn't know it. That's the set-up, isn't it?

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:04.960
Is there anything wrong with it?

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:07.870
No, I think it's lovely. Then if some dark, wet night,

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:09.050
that crown block did fall on him.

00:21:09.070 --> 00:21:09.590
What crown block?

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:11.450
Only sometimes it can't quite make it on its own.

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:12.370
It has to have a little help.

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:14.540
I don't know what you're talking about.

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:15.980
Of course, it doesn't have to be a crown block.

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:18.460
It can be a car backing over and make it fall out of the upstairs window.

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:21.110
Any little thing like that, just so it's a morgue job.

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:22.390
Are you crazy?

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:23.840
Not that crazy.

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:26.630
Goodbye, Mrs. Dietrichson.

00:21:26.750 --> 00:21:27.370
What's the matter?

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:31.320
Look, baby, you can't get away with it.

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:35.460
Well, knock him off, don't you?

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:37.390
It's a horrible thing to say.

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:38.640
What'd you think I was, anyway?

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:40.920
A guy that walks into a good-looking dame's front parlor

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:43.000
and says, good afternoon, I sell accident insurance on husbands?

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:44.600
You got one that's been around too long,

00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:46.120
one you'd like to turn into a little hard cash?

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:47.840
Just give me a smile and I'll help you collect.

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:49.860
Boy, what a dope you must think I am.

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:51.260
I think you're rotten.

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:54.260
I think you're swell, as long as I'm not your husband.

00:21:54.510 --> 00:21:55.190
Get out of here.

00:21:55.610 --> 00:21:56.610
You bet I'll get out of here, baby.

00:21:57.600 --> 00:21:58.500
I'll get out of here but quick.

00:22:09.820 --> 00:22:11.600
So I let her have it straight between the eyes.

00:22:12.140 --> 00:22:13.820
She didn't fool me for a minute, not this time.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:18.760
I knew I had hold of a red-hot poker and the time to drop her was before it burned my hand off.

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:23.150
Stopped her to drive in for a bottle of beer, the one I had wanted all along.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:28.830
Only I wanted it worse now to get rid of the sour taste of her iced tea and everything that went with it.

00:22:30.410 --> 00:22:33.870
I didn't want to go back to the office, so I dropped by a bowling alley at 3rd and Western

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:38.000
and rolled a few lines to get my mind thinking about something else for a while.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:42.580
I didn't feel like eating dinner when I left, and I didn't feel like a show.

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:46.980
So I drove home, put the car away, and went up to my apartment.

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:52.980
It had begun to rain outside, and I watched it get dark and didn't even turn on the light.

00:22:54.240 --> 00:22:55.120
That didn't help me either.

00:22:56.290 --> 00:22:59.410
I was all twisted up inside, and I was still holding on to that red-hot poker.

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:03.890
And right then it came over me that I hadn't walked out on anything at all.

00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:05.970
That the hook was too strong.

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:08.900
That this wasn't the end between her and me.

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:10.830
It was only the beginning.

00:23:12.670 --> 00:23:16.050
So at eight o'clock the bell would ring and I'd know who it was without even having to think.

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:19.450
As if it was the most natural thing in the world.

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:26.670
You got your hat this afternoon.

00:23:27.720 --> 00:23:28.000
Did I?

00:23:28.830 --> 00:23:29.850
Don't you want me to bring it in?

00:23:31.050 --> 00:23:44.800
Sure. Put it on the chair. Where I live.

00:23:45.350 --> 00:23:47.940
In the phone book. It's raining.

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:51.340
Yeah. Peel off your coat and sit down.

00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:55.980
Have a night?

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:58.320
Yes, Long Beach. They're sputting in a new well.

00:23:58.400 --> 00:23:59.240
If only he'd be late.

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:03.430
10.30. It's about time you said you were glad to see me.

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:05.240
I knew you wouldn't leave it like that.

00:24:05.780 --> 00:24:06.260
Like what?

00:24:07.260 --> 00:24:08.100
Like it was this afternoon.

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:11.080
I must have said something that gave you a terribly wrong impression.

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:14.160
You must never think anything like that about me, Walter.

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:15.470
Okay?

00:24:15.630 --> 00:24:17.450
No, it's not okay. Not if you don't believe me.

00:24:18.190 --> 00:24:18.830
What do you want me to do?

00:24:20.330 --> 00:24:24.410
I want you to be nice to me the first time you came to the house.

00:24:25.460 --> 00:24:28.250
Can't be like the first time. Something's happened.

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:29.980
I know it.

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:39.080
I feel as if he was watching me. Not that he cares, not anymore.

00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:42.920
He keeps me on a leash so tight I can't breathe.

00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:45.900
He's in Long Beach, isn't he? Relax.

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:48.010
Maybe I oughtn't to have come.

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:49.590
Maybe you oughtn't.

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:52.160
Want me to go? If you want to.

00:24:52.990 --> 00:24:54.800
Right now? Sure, right now.

00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:12.520
I'm crazy about you, baby.

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:14.560
Crazy about you, Walter.

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:15.960
The fume on your hair.

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:17.080
What's the name of it?

00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:18.720
I don't know.

00:25:18.860 --> 00:25:19.960
I bought it in Ensenada.

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:23.390
You ought to have some of that pink wine to go with it.

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:24.530
The kind that bubbles.

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:26.520
I got his bourbon.

00:25:28.460 --> 00:25:29.600
Bourbon is fine, Walter.

00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:47.000
a couple of glasses, will you? Soda?

00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:48.140
Plain water, please.

00:25:50.370 --> 00:25:52.950
About six months ago, a guy slipped on a cake of soap in his bathtub

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:54.610
and knocked himself cold and was drowned.

00:25:56.110 --> 00:25:57.290
Only he had accident insurance.

00:25:58.060 --> 00:25:59.980
So they had an autopsy and she didn't get away.

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:08.330
who didn't his wife there was a case of a guy who was found shot his wife said he was cleaning

00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:13.820
a gun and his stomach got in the way all she collected was a three to ten stretch in tahatchipee

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:27.280
perhaps it was worth it to her you can carry that as far as the living room here walter who

00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:29.970
takes care of it for you colored woman comes in

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:35.250
a couple of times a week cook your own breakfast squeeze a grapefruit once in a while get the rest

00:26:35.250 --> 00:26:41.190
down the corner drugstore sounds wonderful just strangers beside you you don't know them and you

00:26:41.190 --> 00:26:46.180
don't hate them you don't have to sit across the table and smile at him and that daughter of his

00:26:46.180 --> 00:26:50.530
every morning of your life what daughter oh you mean the little girl on the piano

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:56.650
she lives with us he thinks a lot more of her than he does of me you ever think of a divorce

00:26:56.650 --> 00:26:59.720
he wouldn't give me a divorce suppose because it costs too much money

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:02.160
Hasn't got any money, not since he went into the oil business.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:03.540
What, he had when you married him?

00:27:04.870 --> 00:27:07.580
Yes, he had. And I wanted a home.

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:11.400
Why not? That's not the only reason.

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:14.590
I was his wife's nurse. She was sick a long time.

00:27:15.230 --> 00:27:16.950
When she died, he was terribly broken up.

00:27:17.170 --> 00:27:19.160
I pitied himself.

00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:20.370
Now you hate him.

00:27:20.880 --> 00:27:23.260
Yes, Walter. He's so mean to me.

00:27:24.090 --> 00:27:26.670
Every time I buy a dress or a pair of shoes, he yells his head off.

00:27:26.730 --> 00:27:28.510
He never lets me go anywhere. He keeps me shut up.

00:27:28.610 --> 00:27:29.590
He's always been mean to me.

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:35.690
Even his life insurance all goes to that daughter of his, that Lola.

00:27:36.230 --> 00:27:37.090
Nothing for you at all?

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:39.520
No, and nothing is just what I'm worth to him.

00:27:40.850 --> 00:27:44.320
So you lie awake in the dark and listen to him snore and get ideas?

00:27:45.020 --> 00:27:46.680
Walter, I don't want to kill him. I never did.

00:27:48.280 --> 00:27:50.680
Not even when he gets drunk and slaps my face.

00:27:53.900 --> 00:27:55.080
Only sometimes you wish he was dead.

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:56.680
Perhaps I do.

00:27:56.920 --> 00:27:59.910
And you wish it was an accident, and you had that policy for $50,000.

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:01.380
Is that it?

00:28:01.820 --> 00:28:02.660
Perhaps that, too.

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:07.040
The other night, we drove home from a party.

00:28:07.160 --> 00:28:07.940
He was drunk again.

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:12.100
When we drove into the garage, he just sat there with his head on the steering wheel

00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:13.160
and the motor still running.

00:28:14.770 --> 00:28:17.610
And I thought what it would be like if I didn't switch it off.

00:28:19.010 --> 00:28:21.110
Just closed the garage doors and left them there.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:22.620
I'll tell you what it would be like.

00:28:22.740 --> 00:28:25.060
If you had that accident policy and tried to put a monoxide job,

00:28:25.750 --> 00:28:27.330
we've got a guy in our office named Keyes.

00:28:27.850 --> 00:28:29.970
For him, a setup like that would be just like a slice of railroad...

00:28:30.830 --> 00:28:32.130
In three minutes, he'd know it wasn't an accident.

00:28:32.710 --> 00:28:34.190
In ten minutes, you'd be sitting under the hot lights.

00:28:34.920 --> 00:28:36.860
In a half hour, you'd be signing your name to a confession.

00:28:37.100 --> 00:28:39.240
Well, Walter, I didn't do it, and I'm not going to do it.

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:40.920
Not if there's an insurance company in the picture, baby.

00:28:41.340 --> 00:28:42.900
They know more tricks than a carload of monkeys.

00:28:43.450 --> 00:28:45.250
And if there's a death mixed up in it, you haven't got a prayer.

00:28:46.060 --> 00:28:48.260
They'll hang you just as sure as ten times will buy a dollar.

00:28:49.690 --> 00:28:50.830
And I don't want you to hang, baby.

00:28:54.940 --> 00:28:55.600
I'm thinking about it, will you?

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:05.520
so we just sat there started crying softly like the rain on the window and we didn't say anything

00:29:05.520 --> 00:29:11.480
maybe she had stopped thinking about it but i hadn't i couldn't because it was all tied up

00:29:11.480 --> 00:29:16.000
with something i'd been thinking about for years as long before i ever ran into phyllis

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:22.130
dedrickson because you know how it is keys in this business you can't sleep for trying to figure

00:29:22.130 --> 00:29:25.920
out all the tricks they could pull on you you're like the guy behind the roulette wheel

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:29.960
watching the customers to make sure they don't cook the house and then

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.340
Then, one night, you get to thinking how you could crook the house yourself.

00:29:33.220 --> 00:29:36.260
And do it smart, because you've got that wheel right under your hands.

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:38.050
You know every notch in it by heart.

00:29:39.370 --> 00:29:43.210
And you figure all you need is a plant out front, a shill to put down the bed.

00:29:44.940 --> 00:29:47.900
Suddenly, the doorbell rings and the whole setup is right there in the room with you.

00:29:52.730 --> 00:29:55.160
Keys, I'm not trying to whitewash myself.

00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:59.750
I fought it, only I guess I didn't fight it hard enough.

00:30:01.660 --> 00:30:04.900
The stakes were $50,000, but they were the life of a man, too.

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:11.420
A man who'd never done me any dirt except he was married to a woman he didn't care anything about.

00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:14.120
And I did.

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:27.720
You phoned me?

00:30:29.350 --> 00:30:29.670
Walter.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:35.190
I hate him. I loathe going back to him.

00:30:35.660 --> 00:30:37.060
You believe me, don't you, Walter?

00:30:37.460 --> 00:30:38.100
Sure, I believe you.

00:30:41.510 --> 00:30:44.190
I can't stand it anymore. What if they did hang me?

00:30:44.660 --> 00:30:45.700
They're not going to hang you, baby.

00:30:46.980 --> 00:30:48.280
Better than going on this way.

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:50.860
They're not going to hang you because you're going to do it and I'm going to help you.

00:30:51.730 --> 00:30:52.590
You know what you're saying?

00:30:52.750 --> 00:30:53.530
Sure, I know what I'm saying.

00:30:54.480 --> 00:30:55.720
We're going to do it and we're going to do it right.

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:57.040
And I'm the guy that knows how.

00:30:57.970 --> 00:30:58.990
Walter, you're hurting me.

00:30:59.320 --> 00:30:59.980
There's not going to be any...

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:03.090
Nothing sloppy, nothing weak. It's got to be perfect.

00:31:03.290 --> 00:31:18.900
Call me tomorrow, but not from your house, from a booth.

00:31:19.240 --> 00:31:20.480
Watch your step every single minute.

00:31:21.320 --> 00:31:22.220
This is got to be perfect, you understand?

00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:23.390
Straight down the line.

00:31:26.220 --> 00:31:26.980
Down the line.

00:32:10.360 --> 00:32:14.310
That was it, Keyes. The machinery had started to move, and nothing could stop it.

00:32:15.630 --> 00:32:17.950
First thing we had to do was fix him up with that accident policy.

00:32:19.650 --> 00:32:22.190
I knew he wouldn't buy, but all I wanted was his signature on an application.

00:32:23.960 --> 00:32:25.980
So I had to get him to sign without his knowing what he was signing.

00:32:28.350 --> 00:32:29.970
I wanted another witness besides Phyllis to hear me...

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:30.920
to give him a sales talk.

00:32:32.690 --> 00:32:34.270
I was trying to think with your brain's keys,

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:37.560
because I wanted all the answers ready for all the questions

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:39.360
you were going to spring as soon as Diedrichsen was dead.

00:32:41.450 --> 00:32:42.830
A couple of nights later, I went to the house.

00:32:44.940 --> 00:32:47.280
Fine, except I didn't like the witness Phyllis had brought in.

00:32:49.270 --> 00:32:50.410
It was Diedrichsen's daughter, Lola.

00:32:50.510 --> 00:32:53.750
Made me feel a little queer in the belly.

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:57.500
We were sitting right there in the room playing Chinese checkers,

00:32:59.140 --> 00:32:59.980
as if nothing were going to happen.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:05.030
Suppose you realize, Mr. Diederiksen, that not being an employee,

00:33:05.340 --> 00:33:07.800
you are not covered by the State Compensation Insurance Act.

00:33:08.420 --> 00:33:11.140
The only way you can protect yourself is by having a personal policy of your own.

00:33:11.340 --> 00:33:12.440
Yeah, I know all about that.

00:33:12.540 --> 00:33:14.800
The next thing you'll tell me I need earthquake insurance

00:33:14.800 --> 00:33:17.340
and a lightning insurance and hail insurance.

00:33:17.340 --> 00:33:19.260
If we bought all the insurance they could think of,

00:33:19.380 --> 00:33:21.080
we'd stay broke paying for it, wouldn't we, honey?

00:33:21.300 --> 00:33:25.080
What keeps us broke is you going out and buying five hats at a crack.

