00:46:38.760 --> 00:46:39.920
What about this job I want you for?

00:46:40.320 --> 00:46:42.610
I don't think I want it, Keyes. Thanks, just the same.

00:46:43.570 --> 00:46:46.170
Well, fair enough. Only get this.

00:46:47.000 --> 00:46:49.600
I picked you for the job not because I think you're so darn smart,

00:46:49.960 --> 00:46:52.760
but because I thought you were a shade less dumb than the rest of the outfit.

00:47:00.510 --> 00:47:01.290
Guess I was wrong.

00:47:01.860 --> 00:47:02.920
You're not smarter, Walter.

00:47:03.620 --> 00:47:04.680
You're just a little taller.

00:47:16.800 --> 00:47:17.380
Yes, Keyes.

00:47:18.450 --> 00:47:21.050
Those fates I was talking about had only been stalling me off.

00:47:22.020 --> 00:47:23.100
Now they had thrown the switch.

00:47:23.960 --> 00:47:24.840
The gears had meshed.

00:47:25.670 --> 00:47:27.070
Time for thinking had all run out.

00:47:28.870 --> 00:47:29.850
I wanted my movements accounted for.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:35.100
for up to the last possible moment so when i left the office i put my rate book on the desk as if i

00:47:35.100 --> 00:47:42.560
had forgotten it that was part of my alibi from here on it was a question of following the timetable

00:47:42.750 --> 00:47:49.950
move by move i got home about seven and drove right into the garage this was another item to

00:47:49.950 --> 00:47:58.470
establish my alibi hiya mr net hello charlie how about giving the heap of wash job how soon you're

00:47:58.470 --> 00:47:59.890
gonna want it i got a couple of cars ahead

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:02.720
Any time you get to it, Charlie. I'm staying in tonight.

00:48:04.100 --> 00:48:07.600
Up in my apartment, I called Lou Schwartz, one of the salesmen that shared my office.

00:48:08.590 --> 00:48:11.250
He lived in Westwood, so it was a toll call and there'd be a record of it.

00:48:12.520 --> 00:48:16.300
I told him I'd forgotten my rape book and needed some dope on the public liability bond I was figuring.

00:48:17.500 --> 00:48:20.240
I changed into a navy blue suit like Dietrichson was going to wear.

00:48:21.390 --> 00:48:23.310
Lou Schwartz called me back and gave me a lot of figures.

00:48:24.550 --> 00:48:26.890
I stuffed a hand towel and a roll of adhesive into my pocket

00:48:26.890 --> 00:48:29.590
so I could fake something that looked like a cast on a broken leg.

00:48:31.210 --> 00:48:34.970
Next, I stuck a card inside the telephone box so that it would fall down if the bell rang.

00:48:36.170 --> 00:48:38.330
That way, I'd know if anybody had called me while I was away.

00:48:39.750 --> 00:48:42.470
Then I did the same thing to the doorbell in case anybody came to see me.

00:48:44.690 --> 00:48:47.720
Left the apartment by the service stairs. Nobody saw me.

00:48:49.170 --> 00:48:51.550
I walked all the way from my apartment to the Dietrichsen house.

00:48:52.700 --> 00:48:56.600
I didn't want to take the bus because there was always the chance that someone might remember seeing me on it.

00:48:57.520 --> 00:48:58.440
I was being that careful.

00:49:00.090 --> 00:49:04.440
I could smell that honeysuckle again, only it was even stronger now that it was night.

00:49:10.220 --> 00:49:12.480
I slid the garage door open as quietly as I could.

00:49:13.840 --> 00:49:15.760
She backed the sedan in just the way I told it to.

00:49:16.800 --> 00:49:20.360
I figured it was safer that way, in case he got into the car before she drove it out.

00:49:21.530 --> 00:49:22.790
I got into the back of the car.

00:49:24.040 --> 00:49:25.300
I lay there on the floor and waited.

00:49:26.920 --> 00:49:29.980
All the time I was thinking about that dark street on the way to the station where I...

00:49:30.280 --> 00:49:35.870
do it three honks on the horn that were to be the signal about 10 minutes later they came down

00:49:35.870 --> 00:49:39.930
all right honey yeah i'm all right i'll have the car out in a second

00:50:02.680 --> 00:50:02.900
Thank you.

00:50:40.910 --> 00:50:44.110
Remember what the doctor said, if you get careless, you might end up with a shorter leg.

00:50:44.470 --> 00:50:47.550
So what? I could break the other one and match him up again.

00:50:48.510 --> 00:50:50.830
Makes you feel pretty good to get away from me, doesn't it?

00:50:51.150 --> 00:50:53.890
It's only for four days. I'll be back Monday at the latest.

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:13.780
This is not the right street. Why did you turn here?

00:51:18.330 --> 00:51:19.090
What are you doing that?

00:51:21.700 --> 00:51:23.620
You're honking the horn for me.

00:52:28.090 --> 00:52:29.890
can't tell him I don't want to be help I said don't

00:52:30.670 --> 00:52:32.130
You start as soon as the train leaves.

00:52:33.420 --> 00:52:35.820
When you get to the refinery, turn off the highway onto the dirt road.

00:52:36.100 --> 00:52:39.080
From there, it's exactly eight-tenths of a mile to the dump of the tracks. Remember.

00:52:39.240 --> 00:52:40.320
I remember everything.

00:52:40.960 --> 00:52:44.620
No speeding. You don't want any cops stopping in with him in the back.

00:52:45.910 --> 00:52:47.990
Walter, we've been through all that so many times.

00:52:48.310 --> 00:52:49.690
When you leave the highway, turn off all your lights.

00:52:50.850 --> 00:52:53.890
I'll be back in the observation platform. I'll drop off as close to the spot as I can.

00:52:55.610 --> 00:52:58.340
Let the train pass. Then dim your eyes twice.

00:53:01.210 --> 00:53:02.330
San Francisco train lady?

00:53:02.410 --> 00:53:04.090
Car 9, section 11. Just my husband.

00:53:04.210 --> 00:53:06.010
Car 9, section 11. This way, please.

00:53:14.910 --> 00:53:16.530
Thank you. My husband doesn't like to be held.

00:53:16.730 --> 00:53:17.810
Car number 8, up there.

00:53:23.890 --> 00:53:27.170
Car 9, section 11. Thank you.

00:53:30.900 --> 00:53:32.520
Take good care of yourself with that leg.

00:53:32.660 --> 00:53:33.800
Yeah, you take it easy driving home.

00:53:34.020 --> 00:53:34.740
I'll miss you, honey.

00:53:36.250 --> 00:53:37.310
Section 11, sir.

00:53:39.920 --> 00:53:40.200
Thank you.

00:53:41.190 --> 00:53:41.790
Goodbye, honey.

00:53:45.140 --> 00:53:50.000
Good luck, honey.

00:54:00.120 --> 00:54:01.080
Are you ready to make up my breath right away?

00:54:01.280 --> 00:54:01.720
Yes, sir.

00:54:02.140 --> 00:54:03.660
Going back to the observation car for a smoke.

00:54:03.860 --> 00:54:04.660
Right this way, sir.

00:54:37.930 --> 00:54:52.270
Chair. Thanks. Who's Dan? Going far? Oh, I'll know. My name is Jackson. I'm going all the way to Medford.

00:54:52.540 --> 00:54:59.780
Medford, Oregon. Broken arm once. Darned cast. Itches something fierce, doesn't it?

00:55:00.700 --> 00:55:01.980
I thought I'd go crazy with mine.

00:55:03.080 --> 00:55:04.740
Palo Alto's a nice little town.

00:55:05.870 --> 00:55:06.870
You a Stanford man?

00:55:07.770 --> 00:55:08.970
I, uh, I used to be.

00:55:09.730 --> 00:55:11.850
Uh-oh, I'll bet you left something behind.

00:55:12.550 --> 00:55:13.650
I always do.

00:55:14.280 --> 00:55:17.160
My cigar case, I guess I left it in my overcoat back in the section.

00:55:17.920 --> 00:55:20.680
Would you care to roll yourself a cigarette, mister?

00:55:21.360 --> 00:55:21.760
Dietrichson.

00:55:22.760 --> 00:55:24.560
Oh, thanks, I, uh, I really prefer a cigar.

00:55:25.880 --> 00:55:26.900
Maybe the porter could, uh...

00:55:26.900 --> 00:55:28.940
Well, I could get your cigars for you.

00:55:29.140 --> 00:55:29.920
Be glad to, mister.

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:35.910
It's not too much trouble car nine section left car nine section 11 pleasure

00:56:22.590 --> 00:56:24.520
Take his hat. Watch his back in the tracks.

00:58:07.130 --> 00:58:10.950
On the way back, we went over once more what she was to do at the inquest, if they had one,

00:58:11.600 --> 00:58:13.200
and about the insurance when that came up.

00:58:14.200 --> 00:58:18.060
I was afraid she might go to pieces a little, now that we had done it, but she was perfect.

00:58:18.920 --> 00:58:21.840
No nerves, not a tear, not even a blink of the eyes.

00:58:22.860 --> 00:58:24.660
She dropped me a block from my apartment house.

00:58:26.820 --> 00:58:29.980
Walter, what's the matter? Aren't you going to kiss?

00:58:35.340 --> 00:58:36.700
It's straight down the line, isn't it?

00:58:36.780 --> 00:58:43.400
I love you, Walter.

00:58:45.240 --> 00:58:45.800
I love you, baby.

00:58:59.810 --> 00:58:59.970
It's too much.

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:02.200
Two minutes past 11 as I went up the service stairs again.

00:59:03.110 --> 00:59:04.270
Nobody saw me this time either.

00:59:05.480 --> 00:59:06.780
In the apartment, I checked the bells.

00:59:07.180 --> 00:59:08.060
The cards hadn't moved.

00:59:08.960 --> 00:59:10.120
No calls, no visitors.

00:59:11.620 --> 00:59:12.800
Then I changed my clothes again.

00:59:13.940 --> 00:59:15.280
That left one last thing to do.

00:59:16.630 --> 00:59:17.590
I had to go down to the garage.

00:59:19.090 --> 00:59:20.330
I wanted Charlie to see me again.

00:59:30.860 --> 00:59:33.930
Well, that's all right, Charlie. Just going up to the drugstore to get something to eat.

00:59:34.190 --> 00:59:36.750
I'm working upstairs all that way. Stomach's getting a little sore, I've got.

00:59:37.340 --> 00:59:37.840
Yes, sir.

00:59:40.830 --> 00:59:44.870
That was all there was to it. Nothing had slipped. Nothing had been overlooked.

00:59:45.760 --> 00:59:46.840
There was nothing to give us away.

00:59:48.260 --> 00:59:50.700
And yet, Keyes, as I was walking down the street to the drugstore,

00:59:51.900 --> 00:59:54.080
suddenly it came over me that everything would go wrong.

00:59:55.400 --> 00:59:57.480
It sounds crazy, Keyes, but it's true, so help me.

00:59:58.730 --> 00:59:59.850
I couldn't hear my own footsteps.

01:00:04.510 --> 01:00:05.710
It was the walk of a dead man.

01:00:05.850 --> 01:00:16.440
That was the longest night I ever lived through, Keyes.

01:00:16.960 --> 01:00:19.200
And the next day was worse when the story had broken the papers.

01:00:19.380 --> 01:00:22.420
They started talking about it at the office.

01:00:22.900 --> 01:00:24.920
And the day after that, when you started digging into it,

01:00:26.320 --> 01:00:28.920
I kept my hands in my pockets because I thought they were shaking.

01:00:30.380 --> 01:00:32.360
I put on dark glasses so people couldn't see my eyes.

01:00:32.520 --> 01:00:34.900
Then I took them off again so they wouldn't get to wondering why I wore them.

01:00:36.730 --> 01:00:41.160
Tried to hold myself together, but I feel my nerves pulling me to pieces.

01:00:47.660 --> 01:00:47.940
Keys?

01:00:48.540 --> 01:00:50.400
Come along. The big boss wants to see us.

01:00:50.770 --> 01:00:51.770
The Diederson case?

01:00:51.890 --> 01:00:52.350
Must be.

01:00:53.240 --> 01:00:53.720
Anything wrong?

01:00:53.900 --> 01:00:54.800
Well, the guy is dead.

01:00:55.310 --> 01:00:57.350
We had him assured and he's going to cost us dough. That's always wrong.

01:00:57.490 --> 01:00:59.980
What have they got so far?

01:01:00.090 --> 01:01:04.920
Audiency report. No heart failure, no apoplexy, no predisposing medical cause of any kind.

01:01:05.250 --> 01:01:06.210
Died of a broken neck.

01:01:07.280 --> 01:01:07.920
What's the inquest?

01:01:08.230 --> 01:01:08.970
Had her this morning.

01:01:09.350 --> 01:01:10.830
His wife and daughter made the identification.

01:01:11.850 --> 01:01:14.910
The train people and some of the passengers told how he went through to the observation car.

01:01:15.660 --> 01:01:17.120
It was all over in 45 minutes.

01:01:17.510 --> 01:01:19.070
Verdict? Accidental death.

01:01:20.990 --> 01:01:21.690
What do the police figure?

01:01:22.410 --> 01:01:24.370
He got tangled up in his crutches and fell off the train.

01:01:25.120 --> 01:01:25.880
They're satisfied.

01:01:26.840 --> 01:01:27.600
It's not their dough.

01:01:28.850 --> 01:01:29.350
Come on, Walter.

01:01:31.080 --> 01:01:32.300
All right, thank you very much, gentlemen.

01:01:33.140 --> 01:01:34.580
I believe the legal position is now clear.

01:01:35.250 --> 01:01:36.570
Please stand by. I may need you later.

01:01:37.670 --> 01:01:38.490
Come in, Mr. Keyes.

01:01:39.640 --> 01:01:40.480
You too, Mr. Neff.

01:01:44.970 --> 01:01:47.150
Find this an uncomfortably warm day, Mr. Keyes?

01:01:47.250 --> 01:01:49.670
Oh, I'm sorry, Mr. Norton, but I didn't know this was formal.

01:01:50.090 --> 01:01:50.790
Sit down, gentlemen.

01:01:50.970 --> 01:01:51.290
Thank you.

01:01:51.730 --> 01:01:52.430
Any new developments?

01:01:52.950 --> 01:01:55.630
Well, I just talked to this Jackson long distance up in Metford, Oregon.

01:01:55.710 --> 01:01:56.050
Who's Jackson?

01:01:56.260 --> 01:01:57.860
He's the last man who saw Dietrichson alive.

01:01:58.240 --> 01:01:59.860
They were out on the observation platform together.

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:04.910
talking dietrichson wanted a cigar and so jackson went back to get the cigar case for him when he

01:02:04.910 --> 01:02:08.960
returned to the observation platform no dietrichson well jackson didn't think anything was wrong until

01:02:08.960 --> 01:02:13.140
a wire caught up with the train to santa barbara they found dietrichson's body on the tracks near

01:02:13.140 --> 01:02:19.470
burbank very interesting about the cigar keys anything else no not much dietrichson's secretary

01:02:19.470 --> 01:02:24.030
says she didn't know anything about the policy there's a daughter but all she remembers is

01:02:24.030 --> 01:02:27.770
left talking to her father about accident insurance at the house one night i couldn't

01:02:27.770 --> 01:02:28.330
Tell him at first.

01:02:28.770 --> 01:02:29.970
Mr. Dedrickson opposed it.

01:02:30.060 --> 01:02:31.140
he said he'd think it over.

01:02:31.750 --> 01:02:34.680
Later, I saw him in the oil fields and closed him.

01:02:35.060 --> 01:02:36.500
He signed the application and gave me his check.

01:02:37.180 --> 01:02:39.540
Fine piece of salesmanship that was, Mr. Neff.

01:02:39.740 --> 01:02:41.260
Well, there's no sense in pushing Neff around.

01:02:41.400 --> 01:02:42.920
He's got the best sales record in the office.

01:02:43.440 --> 01:02:44.540
Are your salesman supposed to know

01:02:44.540 --> 01:02:45.900
a customer's gonna fall off the train?

01:02:46.740 --> 01:02:47.440
Fall off a train?

01:02:48.080 --> 01:02:50.140
Are we sure Dietrichson fell off the train?

01:02:50.950 --> 01:02:51.570
I don't get it.

01:02:52.000 --> 01:02:52.900
You don't, Mr. Keyes.

01:02:53.250 --> 01:02:54.630
Then what do you think of this case?

01:02:54.730 --> 01:02:56.490
This policy might cost us a great deal of money.

01:02:56.630 --> 01:02:58.630
As you know, it contains a double indemnity clause.

01:02:58.970 --> 01:02:59.970
Just what is your opinion?

01:03:00.920 --> 01:03:05.720
opinion at all not even a hunch one of those interesting little hunches of yours no not even

01:03:05.720 --> 01:03:12.020
a hunch i'm surprised mr keys i formed a very definite opinion i think i know in fact i know

01:03:12.020 --> 01:03:21.190
i know what happened to didrickson you know you know what i know it was not an accident you say to

01:03:21.190 --> 01:03:28.590
that me well you've got the ball let's see you run with it there's a widespread feeling that just

01:03:28.590 --> 01:03:29.850
because a man has a large

01:03:34.050 --> 01:03:36.140
Come in, please.

01:03:37.410 --> 01:03:40.850
There's a widespread feeling that just because a man has a large office, he must be an idiot.

01:03:42.040 --> 01:03:43.080
I'm having a visitor, if you don't mind.

01:03:43.740 --> 01:03:45.820
No, no, I want you to stay and watch me handle this.

01:03:46.760 --> 01:03:47.600
Mrs. Dietrichson.

01:03:47.760 --> 01:03:54.760
Thank you very much for coming, Mrs. Dietrichson.

01:03:55.310 --> 01:03:56.550
I assure you, I appreciate it.

01:03:57.280 --> 01:03:58.020
Mr. Keyes.

01:03:58.740 --> 01:03:59.380
How do you do?

01:03:59.540 --> 01:03:59.980
Mr. Neck.

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:01.520
I've met Mr. Neff. How do you do?

01:04:02.920 --> 01:04:09.020
May I extend our sympathy in your bereavement?

01:04:09.250 --> 01:04:11.670
I hesitated before asking you to come here so soon after your loss,

01:04:11.750 --> 01:04:14.350
but now that you're here, I hope you won't mind if I plunge straight into business.

01:04:15.160 --> 01:04:16.380
You know why we ask you to come, don't you?

01:04:16.560 --> 01:04:19.260
No, all I know is that your secretary made it sound very urgent.

01:04:19.520 --> 01:04:21.360
Your husband had an accident policy with this company.

01:04:21.910 --> 01:04:23.310
Evidently, you don't know that, Miss Dietrichson.

01:04:23.430 --> 01:04:26.310
No, I remember some talk at the house, but he didn't seem to want it.

01:04:26.590 --> 01:04:28.750
Your husband took the policy out a few days later, Mrs. Dietrichson.

01:04:29.610 --> 01:04:29.970
Your pup...

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:31.800
He'll probably find the policy among his personal effects.

01:04:32.060 --> 01:04:33.840
His safe deposit box hasn't been opened yet.

01:04:34.080 --> 01:04:36.040
It seems a tax examiner has to be present.

01:04:36.340 --> 01:04:39.220
Please, Ms. Dixon, I don't want you to think you're being subjected to any questioning,

01:04:39.360 --> 01:04:40.700
but there are a few things we should like to know.

01:04:41.060 --> 01:04:42.120
What sort of things?

01:04:42.490 --> 01:04:44.450
We have the report of the coroner's inquest.

01:04:44.860 --> 01:04:47.600
Accidental death. We're not entirely satisfied.

01:04:47.880 --> 01:04:50.040
In fact, we're not satisfied at all.

01:04:52.480 --> 01:04:56.840
Frankly, Ms. Dixon, we suspect a suicide.

01:05:00.960 --> 01:05:02.160
I'm sorry. Would you like a glass of water?

01:05:03.000 --> 01:05:09.830
Please. Thank you.

01:05:09.950 --> 01:05:19.300
Has your husband been depressed or moody lately, Miss Dietrichson?

01:05:20.130 --> 01:05:21.650
Did he have financial worries, for instance?

01:05:22.900 --> 01:05:26.040
He was perfectly all right, and I don't know of any financial worries.

01:05:26.330 --> 01:05:28.070
Let us examine this so-called accident.

01:05:28.660 --> 01:05:29.980
First, your husband takes out this policy.

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:36.260
in absolute secrecy why because he doesn't want his family to suspect what he intends to do do what

01:05:36.260 --> 01:05:40.920
next he goes on this trip entirely alone he has to be alone he hobbles all the way out to the

01:05:40.920 --> 01:05:45.470
observation platform very unlikely with his leg in the cast unless he has a very strong reason

01:05:45.910 --> 01:05:51.630
once there he finds he's not alone there's a man there uh what was his name keys name was jackson

01:05:51.630 --> 01:05:57.960
probably still is so he gets rid of this jackson with some flimsy excuse about cigars and then he's

01:05:57.960 --> 01:05:59.590
alone, and then he doesn't.

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:00.360
He does what?

01:06:00.660 --> 01:06:03.440
He jumps. Suicide. In which case the company is not liable.

01:06:04.800 --> 01:06:07.100
You know that, of course. Now, we could go to court.

01:06:07.670 --> 01:06:10.410
I don't know anything. In fact, I don't know why I came here.

01:06:11.730 --> 01:06:14.490
Just a moment, please. I said we could go to court. I didn't say we want to.

01:06:15.000 --> 01:06:17.700
What I want to suggest is a compromise on both sides.

01:06:18.020 --> 01:06:20.520
A settlement for a certain sum, a part of the policy value.

01:06:20.620 --> 01:06:24.040
Don't bother, Mr. Norton. When I came in here, I had no idea you owed me any money.

01:06:24.300 --> 01:06:26.160
You told me you did, then you told me you didn't.

01:06:26.340 --> 01:06:28.820
Now you tell me you want to pay me a part of it, whatever it is.

01:06:28.940 --> 01:06:29.980
You want to bargain with me...

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:34.390
At a time like this, I don't like your insinuations about my husband and I don't like your methods.

01:06:34.850 --> 01:06:37.080
In fact, I don't like you, Mr. Norton.

01:06:37.500 --> 01:06:38.260
Goodbye, gentlemen.

01:06:43.430 --> 01:06:45.650
Nice going, Mr. Norton. You sure carry that ball.

01:06:46.560 --> 01:06:47.880
What do you fumble on the goal line?

01:06:48.480 --> 01:06:51.520
Then you heaved an illegal forward pass and got thrown for a 40-yard loss.

01:06:51.790 --> 01:06:54.190
Now you can't pick yourself up because you haven't got a leg to stand on.

01:06:54.310 --> 01:06:57.130
Why haven't he? She can go to court and we can prove it was suicide.

01:06:57.330 --> 01:06:59.530
Oh, can we? Mr. Norton?

01:07:00.510 --> 01:07:02.330
First thing that struck me was that suicide angle.

01:07:03.110 --> 01:07:05.750
Well, yeah, I dumped it into the waste paper basket just three seconds later.

01:07:06.900 --> 01:07:10.300
You know, you ought to take a look at these statistics on suicide sometime.

01:07:10.720 --> 01:07:12.680
You might learn a little something about the insurance business.

01:07:12.840 --> 01:07:14.980
Mr. Keyes, I was raised in the insurance business.

01:07:15.140 --> 01:07:16.340
Yeah, in the front office.

01:07:16.980 --> 01:07:19.640
Come now, you've never read an actuarial table in your life, have you?

01:07:20.260 --> 01:07:22.000
Why, they've got ten volumes on suicide alone.

01:07:22.560 --> 01:07:26.260
Suicide by race, by color, by occupation, by sex, by seasons of the year, by time of day.

01:07:26.580 --> 01:07:29.700
Suicide, how committed, by poisons, by firearms, by drowning, by leaves.

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:32.380
Suicide by poison, subdivided by types of poison,

01:07:32.500 --> 01:07:36.740
such as corrosive, irritant, systemic, gaseous, narcotic, alkaloid, protein, and so forth.

01:07:36.980 --> 01:07:39.460
Suicide by leaps, subdivided by leaps from high places,

01:07:39.680 --> 01:07:41.440
under the wheels of trains, under the wheels of trucks,

01:07:41.740 --> 01:07:43.780
under the feet of horses, from steamboats.

01:07:44.220 --> 01:07:46.580
But Mr. Norton, of all the cases on record,

01:07:46.700 --> 01:07:50.220
there's not one single case of suicide by leap from the rear end of a moving train.

01:07:50.840 --> 01:07:53.200
And do you know how fast that train was going at the point where the body was found?

01:07:53.870 --> 01:07:54.910
Fifteen miles an hour.

01:07:55.410 --> 01:07:57.550
Now, how can anybody jump off a slow-moving train like that

01:07:57.550 --> 01:07:59.470
with any kind of expectation that he would kill himself?

01:08:00.410 --> 01:08:01.850
No, no soap, Mr. Norton.

01:08:02.350 --> 01:08:05.070
We're sunk, and we'll have to pay through the nose, and you know it.

01:08:05.450 --> 01:08:06.090
May I have this?

01:08:14.940 --> 01:08:16.460
Next time, I'll rent a tuxedo.

01:08:33.770 --> 01:08:36.730
I could have hugged you right then and there, Keyes, you and your statistics.

01:08:37.780 --> 01:08:42.390
You were the only one we were really scared of, and instead you were almost playing on our team.

01:08:43.700 --> 01:08:45.800
That evening when I got home, my nerves had eased off.

01:08:46.670 --> 01:08:50.510
I could feel the ground under my feet again, and it looked like easy going from there on in.

01:08:51.720 --> 01:08:55.940
That hundred thousand bucks looked as safe for Phyllis and me as if we had the check already deposited in the bank.

01:09:03.100 --> 01:09:05.140
Hello. Oh, hello, baby.

01:09:06.400 --> 01:09:09.400
Sure, everything's fine. You were wonderful in Norton's office.

01:09:09.980 --> 01:09:12.040
I felt so funny, I wanted to look at you all the time.

01:09:13.330 --> 01:09:15.320
How'd you think I felt, baby? Where are you?

01:09:15.440 --> 01:09:19.420
The drugstore just a block away. Can I come up?

01:09:19.840 --> 01:09:21.740
Okay, but be careful. Don't let anybody see you.

01:09:49.230 --> 01:09:59.970
He's broken leg guy had a broken

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:02.270
What are you talking about?

01:10:02.490 --> 01:10:03.230
I'm talking about Dietrichsen.

01:10:04.440 --> 01:10:05.680
He had accident insurance, didn't he?

01:10:06.280 --> 01:10:06.400
Yeah.

01:10:06.660 --> 01:10:07.680
Then he broke his leg, didn't he?

01:10:08.280 --> 01:10:08.720
So what?

01:10:08.860 --> 01:10:09.760
And he didn't put in a claim.

01:10:10.280 --> 01:10:11.500
Why didn't he put in a claim?

01:10:11.640 --> 01:10:11.880
Why?

01:10:13.020 --> 01:10:13.880
What are you driving at?

01:10:14.280 --> 01:10:18.990
Walter, I had dinner two hours ago, and he's stuck halfway.

01:10:19.490 --> 01:10:20.870
Little man of yours is acting up again, huh?

01:10:20.950 --> 01:10:22.830
There's something wrong with the Dietrichsen case.

01:10:23.360 --> 01:10:24.420
Why, because he didn't file a claim?

01:10:25.620 --> 01:10:26.660
Maybe he just didn't have time.

01:10:27.220 --> 01:10:28.720
Maybe he just didn't know that he was insured.

01:10:32.400 --> 01:10:33.560
No, no, that couldn't be it.

01:10:33.990 --> 01:10:36.230
You delivered the policy to him personally, didn't you?

01:10:36.350 --> 01:10:36.530
Yeah.

01:10:36.650 --> 01:10:37.170
You got his check?

01:10:37.330 --> 01:10:37.730
Sure, I did.

01:10:39.320 --> 01:10:40.540
Got any bicarbonate of soda?

01:10:41.270 --> 01:10:41.870
No, I haven't.

01:10:43.000 --> 01:10:46.360
Walter, I've been living with this little man for 26 years.

01:10:46.870 --> 01:10:48.090
He's never failed me yet.

01:10:48.170 --> 01:10:49.350
There's got to be something wrong.

01:10:49.850 --> 01:10:50.810
Well, maybe Norton was right.

01:10:51.320 --> 01:10:52.240
Maybe it was suicide.

01:10:52.420 --> 01:10:53.240
No, not suicide.

01:10:54.460 --> 01:10:55.800
But not an accident, either.

01:10:57.140 --> 01:10:57.560
What else?

01:10:58.560 --> 01:10:59.040
Now, look, Walter.

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:03.300
A guy takes out an accident policy that's worth $100,000 if he's killed on the train.

01:11:03.780 --> 01:11:05.320
And two weeks later, he is killed on the train.

01:11:05.520 --> 01:11:09.000
And not in a train accident, mind you, but falling off some silly observation car.

01:11:09.730 --> 01:11:11.850
You know what the mathematical probability of that is?

01:11:12.340 --> 01:11:15.140
One out of, oh, I don't know how many billions.

01:11:16.140 --> 01:11:17.300
And add to that, the broken leg.

01:11:18.360 --> 01:11:20.700
No, it just, it just can't be the way it looks.

01:11:21.460 --> 01:11:23.060
Something has been worked on us.

01:11:24.780 --> 01:11:25.300
Such as what?

01:11:33.420 --> 01:11:36.470
Murder? Don't you have any peppermint or something?

01:11:36.950 --> 01:11:38.510
Oh, sorry. Want a little soda water?

01:11:38.630 --> 01:11:42.140
No, no, no. What do you suspect?

01:11:42.340 --> 01:11:44.100
Well, maybe I like to make things easy for myself,

01:11:44.280 --> 01:11:46.280
but I always tend to suspect the beneficiary.

01:11:46.980 --> 01:11:48.940
You mean the wife? Yeah, that wide-eyed dame

01:11:48.940 --> 01:11:50.200
that just didn't know anything about anything.

01:11:50.650 --> 01:11:52.150
Oh, you're crazy, Keith. She wasn't even on the train.

01:11:52.270 --> 01:11:55.130
I know she wasn't, Walter. I don't claim to know how it was worked

01:11:55.130 --> 01:11:57.130
or who worked it, but all I know is that it was worked.

01:11:58.420 --> 01:11:59.980
I've got to get to a drugstore. This thing feels...

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:07.060
like a hunk of concrete inside me good night walter good night keys see at the office in the

01:12:07.060 --> 01:12:14.190
water yeah i'd like to move in on a right now tonight it wasn't for norton and his tri-pants

01:12:14.190 --> 01:12:20.200
ideas about company policy i'd have the police after her so fast to make a head spin they put

01:12:20.200 --> 01:12:25.340
her through the wringer and bother the things they would squeeze out they haven't got a single

01:12:25.340 --> 01:12:29.880
thing to go on keys oh not too much just 26 years experience all the percentage there is

01:12:30.320 --> 01:12:31.880
There's a hunk of concrete in my stomach.

01:12:32.120 --> 01:12:36.240
Is it one of those things?

01:12:48.490 --> 01:12:48.870
Bye, keys.

01:12:49.740 --> 01:12:50.300
So long, Walter.

01:13:04.380 --> 01:13:05.180
How much does he know?

01:13:06.180 --> 01:13:09.300
He doesn't know anything. It's those stinking hunches of his.

01:13:10.400 --> 01:13:11.780
He can't prove anything, can he?

01:13:11.860 --> 01:13:13.580
Not if we're careful. Not if we don't see each other for a while.

01:13:17.860 --> 01:13:18.640
Not long a while.

01:13:18.840 --> 01:13:19.660
Until this dies down.

01:13:20.800 --> 01:13:23.040
You don't know Keyes. Once he gets his teeth into something, he never lets go.

01:13:23.840 --> 01:13:25.160
He'll investigate you. He'll have you shattered.

01:13:25.280 --> 01:13:26.280
He'll watch you every minute from now on.

01:13:28.280 --> 01:13:28.680
Afraid, baby?

01:13:29.520 --> 01:13:29.980
Yes, I'm...

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:33.980
I'm afraid, but not of keys. I'm afraid of us.

01:13:34.880 --> 01:13:37.750
We're not the same anymore. We did it so we could be together,

01:13:37.890 --> 01:13:39.770
but instead of that, it's pulling us apart, isn't it, Walter?

01:13:39.930 --> 01:13:40.570
What are you talking about?

01:13:40.670 --> 01:13:42.770
And you don't really care whether we see each other or not.

01:13:53.490 --> 01:13:59.440
Hello, Mr. Nath. Hello.

01:14:00.090 --> 01:14:02.520
Lola Dietrichsen. Don't you remember me?

01:14:03.090 --> 01:14:04.250
Yes, yes, of course.

01:14:04.660 --> 01:14:06.100
Could I talk with you just a few minutes?

01:14:06.870 --> 01:14:08.740
Somewhere where we could be alone.

01:14:09.660 --> 01:14:11.580
Oh, yes. Come into my office.

01:14:18.160 --> 01:14:19.880
Something about what happened?

01:14:20.360 --> 01:14:23.960
Yes, Mr. Neff. It's about my father's death.

01:14:25.210 --> 01:14:26.210
I'm terribly sorry, Miss Dietrichsen.

01:14:30.440 --> 01:14:32.850
Oh, Lou, do you mind if I use the office alone for a few minutes?

01:14:33.030 --> 01:14:33.930
No, it's all yours, daughter.

01:14:42.010 --> 01:14:46.920
Look at me, Miss Jeanette. I'm not crazy. I'm not hysterical. I'm not even crying.

01:14:48.050 --> 01:14:50.130
But I have the awful feeling that something's wrong.

01:14:50.450 --> 01:14:55.110
And I had that same feeling once before, when my mother died.

01:14:55.870 --> 01:14:56.450
When your mother died?

01:14:56.760 --> 01:14:59.160
We were at Lake Alley. That was six years ago.

01:14:59.400 --> 01:14:59.960
We had a cab.

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:01.820
It was winter and very cold.

01:15:02.320 --> 01:15:03.920
My mother was very sick with pneumonia.

01:15:04.340 --> 01:15:06.090
She had a nurse with her.

01:15:06.830 --> 01:15:08.470
There were just three of us in the cabin.

01:15:09.900 --> 01:15:11.960
One night I got up and went into my mother's room.

01:15:12.520 --> 01:15:13.880
She was delirious with fever.

01:15:14.720 --> 01:15:17.340
All the bed covers were on the floor and the windows were wide open.

01:15:18.560 --> 01:15:19.480
The nurse wasn't in the room.

01:15:19.660 --> 01:15:21.580
I ran and covered my mother up as quickly as I could.

01:15:21.940 --> 01:15:23.480
Just then I heard the door open behind me.

01:15:25.300 --> 01:15:28.870
The nurse stood there. She didn't say a word.

01:15:29.620 --> 01:15:29.960
There was a...

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:31.100
Look in her eyes.

01:15:31.820 --> 01:15:32.720
I'll never forget.

01:15:35.340 --> 01:15:38.220
Two days later, my mother was dead.

01:15:42.080 --> 01:15:42.980
Who that nurse was?

01:15:44.450 --> 01:15:44.630
Who?

01:15:45.180 --> 01:15:45.500
Alice.

01:15:46.330 --> 01:15:48.610
I tried to tell my father, but I was just a kid then.

01:15:48.790 --> 01:15:49.620
He couldn't listen to me.

01:15:50.310 --> 01:15:53.120
Six months later, she married him, and I kind of talked to myself

01:15:53.120 --> 01:15:55.160
out of the idea she could have done anything like that.

01:15:56.470 --> 01:15:57.690
But now it's all back again.

01:15:57.940 --> 01:15:59.840
Now that something's happened to my father, too.

01:16:01.050 --> 01:16:04.500
You're not making sense, Miss Diederson. Your father fell off a train.

01:16:05.240 --> 01:16:07.580
Two days before he fell off that train, what was Phyllis doing?

01:16:08.080 --> 01:16:11.620
She was in a room in front of a mirror with a black hat on and pinning a black veil to it.

01:16:11.990 --> 01:16:14.090
As if she couldn't wait to see how she would look in mourning.

01:16:15.520 --> 01:16:18.890
You've had a pretty bad job, Miss Diederson. Aren't you just imagining these things?

01:16:19.420 --> 01:16:23.110
Caught her eyes in the mirror. They had that look in them they had before my mother died.

01:16:23.930 --> 01:16:24.990
That same look.

01:16:26.210 --> 01:16:29.780
You don't like your stepmother, do you? Isn't it just because she is your stepmother?

01:16:30.190 --> 01:16:32.430
Because she did it. She did it for the money.

01:16:32.750 --> 01:16:34.450
Only you're not going to pay her, are you, Mr. Neff?

01:16:34.800 --> 01:16:37.560
She's not going to get away with it this time because I'm going to speak up.

01:16:37.920 --> 01:16:39.240
I'm going to tell everything I know.

01:16:39.500 --> 01:16:40.580
You'd better be careful saying things.

01:16:40.580 --> 01:16:58.380
I'm not afraid. I'm not mean to act like this.

01:17:00.480 --> 01:17:03.220
All this that you've been telling me, who else have you told?

01:17:03.660 --> 01:17:04.120
No one.

01:17:04.960 --> 01:17:05.620
How about your stepmother?

01:17:06.340 --> 01:17:09.170
Of course, I've moved out.

01:17:11.270 --> 01:17:12.440
I'm not living at home anymore.

01:17:13.320 --> 01:17:14.960
And you haven't told that boyfriend of yours, Siketi?

01:17:15.760 --> 01:17:17.020
I'm not seeing him anymore.

01:17:17.680 --> 01:17:18.380
We had a fight.

01:17:20.630 --> 01:17:21.170
Where are you living now?

01:17:22.660 --> 01:17:24.320
My sofa in an apartment in Hollywood.

01:17:25.520 --> 01:17:27.080
Four walls and you just sit and look at them, huh?

01:17:29.470 --> 01:17:29.970
Yes, ma'am.

01:17:33.230 --> 01:17:36.230
So that evening, I took her to dinner at a Mexican restaurant

01:17:36.230 --> 01:17:38.190
down on O'Bara Street, where nobody would see us.

01:17:39.200 --> 01:17:40.140
I wanted to cheer her up.

01:17:41.570 --> 01:17:44.150
The next day was Sunday, and we went for a ride down to the beach.

01:17:44.950 --> 01:17:46.990
She'd loosened up a bit. She was even laughing.

01:17:48.120 --> 01:17:50.960
I had to make sure that she wouldn't tell that stuff about Phyllis to anybody else.

01:17:51.420 --> 01:17:53.680
It was dynamite, whether it was true or not.

01:17:54.740 --> 01:17:56.180
I had no chance to talk to Phyllis.

01:17:56.460 --> 01:17:57.940
You were watching her like a hawk, Keyes.

01:17:58.740 --> 01:17:59.980
I couldn't even phone her because I was...

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:05.460
afraid you had the wires tapped. Monday morning there was a note on my desk that you wanted to

01:18:05.460 --> 01:18:13.510
see me, Keyes. For a minute I wondered if it could be about Lola. It was worse. Outside your door was

01:18:13.510 --> 01:18:29.560
the last guy in the world I wanted to see. Come in. Come in, Walter. Keyes?

01:18:31.020 --> 01:18:31.760
I want to ask you something.

01:18:32.700 --> 01:18:34.260
After all the years we've known each other,

01:18:34.400 --> 01:18:35.880
do you mind if I make a rather blunt statement?

01:18:36.260 --> 01:18:36.740
About what?

01:18:36.900 --> 01:18:37.320
About me.

01:18:38.560 --> 01:18:40.460
Walter, I'm a very great man.

01:18:41.040 --> 01:18:41.180
Yeah?

01:18:41.260 --> 01:18:42.100
This Dietrich's in business.

01:18:42.620 --> 01:18:45.270
It's murder, and murders don't come any media.

01:18:46.160 --> 01:18:48.680
As fancy a piece of homicide as anybody ever ran into.

01:18:49.080 --> 01:18:50.740
Smart, tricky, almost perfect.

01:18:50.960 --> 01:18:54.150
But I think Papa has it all figured out.

01:18:54.820 --> 01:18:58.190
Figured out and wrapped up in tissue paper with pink ribbons on it.

01:18:58.730 --> 01:18:59.330
Go ahead, I'm listening.

01:19:00.160 --> 01:19:04.050
You know what? That guy, Dietrichsen, was never on the train.

01:19:05.920 --> 01:19:06.260
He wasn't?

01:19:06.490 --> 01:19:07.370
No, he wasn't.

01:19:09.560 --> 01:19:13.970
Now, look, Walter, you can't be sure of killing a man by throwing him off a train that's going 15 miles an hour.

01:19:15.070 --> 01:19:18.870
The only way you can be sure is to kill him first and then throw his body on the tracks.

01:19:19.670 --> 01:19:24.260
Now, that would mean either killing him on the train or, and this is where it really gets fancy,

01:19:24.730 --> 01:19:26.470
you kill him somewhere else and put him on the tracks.

01:19:26.960 --> 01:19:27.720
Two possibilities.

01:19:28.490 --> 01:19:29.970
And I personally buy the second.

01:19:31.850 --> 01:19:32.690
Way ahead of me, Keyes.

01:19:33.490 --> 01:19:34.430
Walter was like this.

01:19:35.240 --> 01:19:36.020
They killed the guy.

01:19:36.670 --> 01:19:38.810
The wife and somebody else.

01:19:39.610 --> 01:19:40.670
Then the somebody else took the crutches

01:19:40.670 --> 01:19:41.870
and went on the train as Dietrichson.

01:19:42.560 --> 01:19:43.780
Then the somebody else jumped off.

01:19:44.420 --> 01:19:45.580
Then they put the body on the tracks

01:19:45.580 --> 01:19:46.480
where the train had passed.

01:19:47.080 --> 01:19:48.140
An impersonation, see?

01:19:49.110 --> 01:19:49.790
Singed to work.

01:19:50.440 --> 01:19:51.100
Because it was night.

01:19:51.570 --> 01:19:52.550
Very few people were about.

01:19:52.770 --> 01:19:53.930
They had the crutches to stare at.

01:19:54.320 --> 01:19:55.940
They never really looked at the man at all.

01:19:56.840 --> 01:19:57.740
Fancy, all right, Keyes.

01:19:57.800 --> 01:19:59.030
But maybe it's a little too fancy.

01:19:59.150 --> 01:19:59.430
Is it?

01:20:00.120 --> 01:20:02.200
I tell you, it all fits together like a watch.

01:20:03.520 --> 01:20:04.760
Now, let's see what we have in the way of proof.

01:20:05.630 --> 01:20:08.110
The only guy who really got a good look at this supposed Dietrichson

01:20:08.110 --> 01:20:09.590
is sitting right outside my office.

01:20:10.270 --> 01:20:11.930
I took the trouble to bring him down here from Oregon.

01:20:15.620 --> 01:20:16.280
Come here, Mr. Jackson.

01:20:16.560 --> 01:20:17.820
Yes, sir, Mr. Keyes.

01:20:18.500 --> 01:20:19.900
These are fine cigars you smoke.

01:20:20.060 --> 01:20:20.640
Two for a quarter.

01:20:20.820 --> 01:20:21.400
That's what I said.

01:20:21.880 --> 01:20:23.120
Well, did you study those photographs?

01:20:23.400 --> 01:20:23.980
Yes, indeed.

01:20:24.700 --> 01:20:26.160
I studied them thoroughly, very thoroughly.

01:20:26.520 --> 01:20:27.280
Have you made up your mind?

01:20:27.460 --> 01:20:29.320
Mr. Keyes, I'm the Medford man, Medford, Oregon.

01:20:29.560 --> 01:20:29.980
Up in Medford...

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:31.780
We take our time making up our minds.

01:20:31.880 --> 01:20:33.580
Well, we're not in Medford now. We're in a hurry.

01:20:34.020 --> 01:20:34.460
Let's have it.

01:20:35.220 --> 01:20:37.120
Are these photographs of the late Mr. Dietrichson?

01:20:37.320 --> 01:20:37.540
Yes.

01:20:38.780 --> 01:20:39.820
Then my answer is no.

01:20:40.160 --> 01:20:40.740
What do you mean, no?

01:20:41.010 --> 01:20:42.730
I mean, this is not the man that was on the train.

01:20:43.400 --> 01:20:44.060
Will you swear to that?

01:20:44.460 --> 01:20:46.180
Mr. Keyes, I'm a Medford man, Medford, Oregon.

01:20:46.640 --> 01:20:47.400
If I say it, I mean it.

01:20:47.460 --> 01:20:48.480
If I mean it, of course, I'll swear it.

01:20:49.360 --> 01:20:50.900
Hey, Walter, there's your proof.

01:20:51.280 --> 01:20:52.680
Oh, this is Mr. Neff, one of our salesmen.

01:20:52.780 --> 01:20:53.960
Oh, pleased to meet you, Mr. Neff.

01:20:54.040 --> 01:20:54.400
Please, indeed.

01:20:54.660 --> 01:20:54.980
How are you?

01:20:55.120 --> 01:20:55.880
Very fine, thank you.

01:20:55.960 --> 01:20:56.460
Never was better.

01:20:56.620 --> 01:20:57.460
Sit down, Mr. Jackson.

01:20:57.460 --> 01:20:59.900
just how would you describe them?

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:05.460
you saw on the observation platform well i'm pretty sure he was a younger man about 10 or 15

01:21:05.460 --> 01:21:10.130
years younger than the man in these photographs putriksen was about 50 wasn't he walter 51

